boba NEWS
=========

0.10.1 (pre-alpha)
------------------

stream: the commit path validates UTF-8 and owns the attribute state

  Two faults in the byte path, both of which made the committed
  scrollback depend on something the app cannot control.

  Chunking. A child's output arrives in whatever chunks the read
  returned. A truncated multibyte tail was dropped (`ab\xc3` + `\xa9z`
  printed `ab`, then a stray continuation byte as if it were a
  character), and a lead byte followed by a non-continuation swallowed
  that byte. Each sequence is now validated against the Unicode tables
  before it is staged: a tail cut off by the end of a message is carried
  (at most three bytes of row state) and completed by the next message,
  and an ill-formed sequence prints U+FFFD once per maximal subpart,
  never swallowing the byte that broke it. Where the caller cut its
  bytes cannot change what reaches the terminal.

  Attribute state. boba passed SGR through verbatim and never reset
  between writes, so a colour the app left open bled into the app's own
  live region — the transcript bytes and the frame repaint leave in the
  same write. The CR replace kept a replaced frame's SGR by scanning the
  bytes out of staging, which is a copy of the state, not the state:
  attributes set between the CR and the next glyph, or in an earlier
  batch, could not be expressed at all. The transcript now tracks the
  set the stream has left in effect — a byte log of the sequences since
  the last reset, capped at a kilobyte, where the newest sequence wins.
  A committed write ends in the default state, a write that continues an
  open row restates the state first, a CR replace restates the state as
  of its CR, and clear() drops it with the rest of the old chat. The
  live frame records nothing; its attributes are its own, repainted
  every frame.

  Builds on 0.10.0, which shipped the carriage-return reading of the
  system stream (see below).

0.10.0 (pre-alpha)
------------------

stream: a carriage return can replace the open row

  Terminal-style output is CR-driven: a child's progress meter writes
  `10%\r50%\r100%\n`, and the system stream turned every bare CR into a row
  break — three rows and no animation, with no way for the app to take the
  bytes back. A hold in the app (wait for a newline, a settle timer) costs
  every user latency and shows no progress while the command runs.

  The transcript now reads a bare CR as a carriage return when the message
  says so. The CR marks the open row and the next glyph or tab replaces it,
  in the same one-write paint the runtime already uses to extend that row;
  a CR alone leaves the last frame standing, `CR LF` is a line break (also
  across messages), and the SGR of a replaced frame is kept, because a
  terminal's CR leaves attribute state alone. Rows already ended are never
  touched, and nothing is held back.

  Added:
    - tui_msg_stream_text_ex() + TUI_STREAM_TEXT_CR_REPLACES, and
      TuiStreamMsg.flags to carry the reading
    - tui_runtime_transcript_write_ex() + TUI_TRANSCRIPT_REPLACE_OPEN_ROW

  Unchanged: tui_msg_stream_text and tui_runtime_transcript_write keep
  CR = row break, byte for byte.

0.9.0 (pre-alpha)
-----------------

textinput: read access to the history ring

  The ring was write-only: an app could add lines, but not read them
  back, so a shell that persists history had to keep its own copy — and
  that copy could disagree with what Up/Down actually walk.

  Added:
    - tui_textinput_history_count(): how many lines the ring holds
    - tui_textinput_history_entry(): a line by index, 0 = the most
      recently added, count - 1 = the oldest. Borrowed: valid until the
      next history_add() or set_history_size().

0.8.0 (pre-alpha)
-----------------

textinput: content larger than the terminal, and resize

  TuiTextInput no longer assumes its content fits on screen. It paints
  through a window over its visual rows, never emits a row outside the
  screen (alt-screen or inline) once it knows the terminal size, keeps the
  cursor inside the window, and reacts to terminal resizes.

  This is an INTERFACE RESET, not an additive change. boba is pre-alpha and
  the old surface had accumulated six overlapping size/placement setters, a
  `terminal_row == 0` sentinel doing two jobs, and a public struct whose
  dead fields (`height`, `scroll_offset`) had never been read.

  Added:
    - TuiTextInputGeometry (one struct) + tui_textinput_set_geometry() /
      tui_textinput_geometry(): `sizing` (TUI_TEXTINPUT_NATURAL / _FIXED /
      _FLOW), `place` (TUI_TEXTINPUT_INLINE / _AT_ROW) + `row`, `width`,
      `rows`, `min_rows`, `reserve_above`, `reserve_below`, `side_margin`,
      `scrolloff`
    - tui_textinput_set_placement(), tui_textinput_set_rows(),
      tui_textinput_set_wrap() as one-line setters over the same struct
    - TuiTextInputScroll + tui_textinput_scroll_state() (both axes)
    - tui_textinput_total_rows(), tui_textinput_visible_rows(),
      tui_textinput_cursor_visual()
    - tui_textinput_has_mark(), tui_textinput_mark(),
      tui_textinput_has_selection(), tui_textinput_selection_range(),
      tui_textinput_undo_depth()
    - PgUp / PgDn move the cursor by visible_rows - 1
    - TUI_MSG_WINDOW_SIZE is consumed by the component (before the focus
      gate, so a resize while blurred still moves its window)

  Changed:
    - TuiTextInput is opaque. Read state through the accessors above.
    - tui_textinput_set_soft_wrap(int) -> tui_textinput_set_wrap(TuiTextInputWrap)
    - TuiTextInputConfig: `width`/`height` -> `rows` + `geometry`
    - TuiTextInputConfig.geometry is a seed for the model, not a
      declaration the component re-reads
    - `get_height()` semantics were ambiguous, so the name is gone:
      visible_rows() is H, total_rows() is the natural height
    - CLIP mode scrolls every row under ONE horizontal window (previously
      only the cursor's row moved sideways)
    - single-line and multi-line no longer render differently: one emitter

  Removed (compile errors, not deprecations):
    - tui_textinput_set_terminal_width(), tui_textinput_set_terminal_row()
    - tui_textinput_get_height()
    - tui_textinput_set_soft_wrap()
    - tui_textinput_set_focus() (send tui_msg_focus()/tui_msg_blur())
    - tui_textinput_set_prompt_color() (use the TuiStyle prompt setters)

  Fixed:
    - alt-screen: rows past the bottom were emitted as CSI r;1H with
      r > term_h, so terminals clamped them onto the last row and overdrew
      it; the surplus-erase loop had the same problem
    - inline: a frame taller than the terminal stranded its top rows in
      scrollback and repainted from mid-frame (duplicated / walking prompt)
    - resizes in either dimension now re-lay-out and keep the cursor
      visible; no stale or duplicated rows

  Note on those clamps: they need the terminal size, and the input learns it
  from one place only — a TUI_MSG_WINDOW_SIZE that reaches its update(). The
  runtime sends that to the ROOT component, so a parent that composes the
  input must forward it down, in every sizing mode and both placements. An
  input that never sees one paints where it was told (an AT_ROW box runs off
  the bottom of the screen; an inline frame is not capped), exactly as
  before this version.

  Internal:
    - src/fit.h: tui_fit_rows()/tui_fit_cols(), shared fit arithmetic for
      every component that paints a box of rows
    - runtime: inline cursor-up is clamped to the screen, erasing from the
      frame top down when the window shrank under an older frame
